2
00:00:11.926 --> 00:00:14.966
Hi, I'm from UC Berkeley
and this is Workhorse.

3
00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:18.566
A humanoid that learns whole-body
loco-manipulation from human data.

4
00:00:18.786 --> 00:00:20.746
And this work was mainly done by Songbo,

5
00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:24.366
an undergrad student
at Tsinghua University.

6
00:00:24.466 --> 00:00:26.206
And he is looking for a PhD position.

7
00:00:26.366 --> 00:00:29.406
So if you like this work,
feel free to send an email to him.

8
00:00:31.246 --> 00:00:32.406
This is the demo.

9
00:00:32.746 --> 00:00:37.946
It's currently fully autonomous
and can climb a suitcase,

10
00:00:37.986 --> 00:00:40.966
catch a thrown ball,
or throw balls with its whole-body.

11
00:00:41.446 --> 00:00:43.866
And all of these come
from the human data only.

12
00:00:44.066 --> 00:00:44.966
With no technology.

13
00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:45.806
No data operations.

14
00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:47.386
And there are two policies.

15
00:00:47.666 --> 00:00:51.846
One is the visual planner
and gives the command

16
00:00:51.886 --> 00:00:54.646
and the whole-body tracker follows it.

17
00:00:56.846 --> 00:01:00.986
So we collect data
by having a person record

18
00:01:01.026 --> 00:01:04.166
the task with five trackers
and a chest camera.

19
00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:10.206
And we keep only these five link
poses as the interface between...

20
00:01:15.426 --> 00:01:17.166
Between the two policies.

21
00:01:17.586 --> 00:01:22.286
The robot follows human poses
directly with no retargeting.

22
00:01:24.486 --> 00:01:30.086
The same poses also work on the second
humanoid, other embodiment like the H2.

23
00:01:33.146 --> 00:01:34.146
So...

24
00:01:39.126 --> 00:01:43.806
In the training, we need to do
some command augmentations...

25
00:01:43.846 --> 00:01:48.086
To bridge the gap between
the planner and the tracker.

26
00:01:49.286 --> 00:01:55.066
If we only train on a clean command,
it's fragile under drift and pushes.

27
00:01:55.456 --> 00:01:57.236
...and become discontinuous.

28
00:01:57.336 --> 00:02:02.136
So we try to mimic this kind of planner
behaviors due to the drifting,

29
00:02:02.176 --> 00:02:04.916
due to the execution
errors during trainings

30
00:02:04.956 --> 00:02:08.736
when training these low-level trackers.

31
00:02:08.896 --> 00:02:13.436
So you can see this yellow ghost
keeps drifting and, at each replan,

32
00:02:13.476 --> 00:02:19.196
resets to the original reference motion.

33
00:02:22.316 --> 00:02:29.076
And the planner reads the history
of images and poses and the collected data

34
00:02:29.116 --> 00:02:32.796
from humans is clean but at deployment,

35
00:02:32.836 --> 00:02:35.756
the history drifts because
of the tracking errors.

36
00:02:36.576 --> 00:02:40.896
So we drift the history in training
for both the pose and image.

37
00:02:42.196 --> 00:02:44.136
The action chunks stay unchanged.

38
00:02:44.556 --> 00:02:46.236
So the action remains optimal.

39
00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:50.696
So for the tracking errors,
we drift the command.

40
00:02:50.916 --> 00:02:52.756
For the planner, we drift the history.

41
00:02:54.956 --> 00:02:58.976
And in deployments,
we need to consider latencies.

42
00:02:59.396 --> 00:03:01.396
We have some initials.

43
00:03:01.436 --> 00:03:07.116
You have some guess on the latency of each
sensors and we label it with timestamp.

44
00:03:07.236 --> 00:03:10.656
We do the plan from the latest image.

45
00:03:11.656 --> 00:03:17.696
And then we predict the action
chunks after the image.

46
00:03:17.736 --> 00:03:23.076
And of course, the first
actions of the action chunk

47
00:03:23.116 --> 00:03:28.376
will be already past because
it's from the last image.

48
00:03:28.976 --> 00:03:36.216
But we have some action chunks
to smooth the plan trajectories.

49
00:03:36.776 --> 00:03:40.456
And these two policies
work smoothly together.

50
00:03:42.376 --> 00:03:47.676
And another important point is we do
some evaluation in simulation.

51
00:03:47.716 --> 00:03:51.076
We do some evaluation
to be able to do some science.

52
00:03:51.376 --> 00:03:57.556
Like for most
of the navigation's behavior,

53
00:03:57.596 --> 00:04:03.316
it can be tested in simulation very well,
with Gaussian splatting for the scene.

54
00:04:03.656 --> 00:04:11.076
And since our manipulation is mainly on
non-prehensile and there are no soft body,

55
00:04:11.116 --> 00:04:13.656
no very dense manipulation,

56
00:04:13.696 --> 00:04:17.156
so we are actually also
able to test this kind

57
00:04:17.196 --> 00:04:21.916
of manipulation's behaviors
